  <div class="abstract">
    <p data-label="Summary">A Combination block makes one colour image from the images of several workflows: one
    workflow per filter of a monochrome camera, or the channels of one colour image shot through a dual-band filter.
    What it does is written in a recipe, a short file made of a few steps from a fixed list: the sky of every channel
    brought to zero, a linear match of the channels to a reference, an optional continuum subtraction. A recipe then
    makes red, green and blue from the channels, either with a hue per filter or with a matrix. Four recipes give the
    blocks S-H-O, H-O, RGB and LRGB.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="chain" aria-label="Position in the graph">
    <li><span>Stacking</span></li><li><span>Denoise</span></li>
    <li class="here"><span>Combination</span></li><li><span>Color</span></li><li><span>Stars/background</span></li>
  </ol>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>What goes in, what comes out</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>One block per recipe.</b> The block is named after its recipe: S-H-O, H-O, RGB or LRGB. The
    generic name, Combination, stands for any other recipe.</aside>
    <p>A combination is the first block of its own workflow. Each of its inputs, called a port (S II, Hα and O III for
    S-H-O; R, G and B for RGB), is wired to one workflow, one per filter. The block reads that workflow’s Output: its
    linear image, with the sky taken off. When the colour is made on separate images, a port may instead read the starless
    image or the stars alone of the workflow’s <a href="/blocks/stars/">Stars/background</a> block. Any workflow may feed any port. A port with no
    workflow is empty and the block waits for it.</p>
    <p>Each port reads one channel of its image: the luminance of an image, its red, green or blue, or the mean of green
    and blue. A monochrome image is read as its grey. The mean of green and blue is the O III of a dual-band filter on a
    colour sensor, whose green and blue pixels both see the 501 nm line.</p>
    <p>The block writes a stack, which its own Output block renders as it renders any stack. It handles no star: a
    Stars/background block placed after it does, and so does the Combine layers block in <a href="/blocks/post/">Post</a>. The valid area of the
    result is where every input has data. Its plate solution is that of the first input that has one.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>One grid for all the channels</h2>
    <p>The block aligns nothing. Its inputs must be on one pixel grid, that of the first port. The channels of one
    colour image are. Images of other workflows are, once an Align on block, placed after their <a href="/blocks/stacking/">Stacking</a>, has brought
    them onto the first one’s grid.</p>
    <p>Align on has two methods. <strong>Stars</strong>, the default, matches triangles of stars
    (Valdes et al.,<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> Groth,<a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> with the
    implementation of Astroalign<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>), then fits the cubic polynomial of <a href="/blocks/registration/">Registration</a> on
    every star matched. <strong>Plate</strong> reprojects the reference grid through the plate solutions of both images,
    when they carry one. If the inputs are not on one grid, the block refuses them and names the ports concerned:
    “Hα is not on the grid of S II: place Align on after its Stacking”.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>The steps of a recipe</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Why the sky first.</b> An input’s own sky level is the median of the whole frame, nebulosity
    included. It differs from one filter to the other by what the nebula adds. In one set, a few tenths of a percent of
    offset in the S II became a red or cyan cast over the whole sky, 8 times the sky’s own noise.</aside>
    <p>A recipe lists steps in order, from three operations. The first is always the background neutralisation.</p>
    <h3>Background neutralisation</h3>
    <p>An empty sky must map to (0, 0, 0) whatever the hues. Each channel’s sky level is therefore measured on the same
    pixels in every channel and subtracted in the linear data. Rector et al.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a> set the
    background of each filter’s image to zero before the layers are coloured and added. The sky pixels are the faintest
    fifth of the blocks of 16 × 16 pixels, judged in all channels at once, each in units of its own spread. The level
    is the median of these pixels after a 3σ clipping.</p>
    <h3>Linear match</h3>
    <p>Each channel is fitted by least squares to a reference channel, reference = a × channel + b, on the pixels where
    the reference lies between 1e-7 and 0.875 of the sensor’s full scale. This is the linear match of Siril and of its RGB
    compositing tool,<a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a> with the bounds of that tool. The offset b moves the sky, which
    is taken back to zero after the fit. The reference is the H-alpha for narrow-band recipes, the strongest signal, so
    that a faint O III weighs as much as a strong H-alpha. It is the red for RGB and the luminance for LRGB, the top layer
    of the compositing tool.<a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a> A fit with a negative slope is refused: the channels do
    not show the same field.</p>
    <h3>Continuum subtraction</h3>
    <p>A narrow-band filter records its line and the continuum under it. A broadband filter around the line records the
    continuum and a small share of the line. The stellar method<a class="cite" href="#ref-6">[6]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-7">[7]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8">[8]</a>
    takes the stars of the field, which have no line emission: the ratio of their fluxes in the two images is the scale
    of the continuum. The line image minus the broadband image times that scale gives the line. FLOAT measures the
    stars by aperture photometry, a circle whose radius is 1.5 times the stars’ full width at half maximum, the local sky taken in an annulus from
    1.25 to 2 radii, and keeps the median of their ratios. A star with a neighbour in its annulus, a saturated star and a
    star under 10 times its flux error are left out. At least 5 stars are needed. The line is then divided by the scale,
    so that a star is equal in both images. A recipe may also give the scale as a number.</p>
    <p>After the steps, the weights of the channels (1 by default) scale each channel.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>A hue per filter, lightness and colour</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>The hue palette.</b> Tinting each channel with its hue and adding the layers is, on linear
    data, a matrix whose columns are the colours of the hues. Each column is weighted so that equal channels give a grey.
    Hues with no grey between them are refused.</aside>
    <p>The narrow-band recipes give each filter a hue, in degrees on the colour wheel (0 red, 120 green, 240 blue).
    Each filter’s greyscale image is tinted with its hue and the layers are added, as Rector et al. describe.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>
    The colour balance is solved so that a single filter’s signal has exactly its own hue and a neutral input stays
    neutral. Three hues solve it exactly. Two hues, for H-O, are solved by least squares, which is exact when the hues
    face each other. A recipe may give a matrix instead: three rows (red, green, blue), one number per channel.</p>
    <p>The default hues of S-H-O are taken from five public S-H-O composites of the Hubble teams
    (ESA/Hubble heic1501a, opo9544a, heic1007a, heic1520a, heic0707a), read in the perceptual space Oklch on their
    nebula pixels: S II 15.5°, H-alpha 31.4° and O III 202.2° on the colour wheel of the linear data, which land on the
    warm areas’ lower quartile, the warm areas’ median and the blue areas’ median in <a href="/blocks/post/">Post</a>’s default display. The defaults
    of H-O are the usual mapping of a dual-band filter: H-alpha red (0°), O III cyan (180°).</p>
    <p>A narrow-band image is composed as lightness and colour, the way an LRGB is.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">[9]</a></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The lightness</strong> is the mean of the channels, each weighted by 1 over the square of its sky noise
      (the least noisy image weighs most), or an equal mean. Equal channels give that same value: a neutral source keeps its
      level.</li>
      <li><strong>The colour</strong> is each pixel’s colour ratios to its own luminance, smoothed. The eye resolves colour
      more coarsely than lightness,<a class="cite" href="#ref-10">[10]</a> so the lightness keeps every detail. In Auto,
      the smoothing radius is where the noisiest channel’s noise, averaged by the Gaussian, comes down to the lightness’s
      own; below half a pixel it is 0. The radius is shown in the results.</li>
      <li><strong>The noise floor.</strong> Where the colour’s luminance is under 3 times its noise, measured on the sky
      pixels, the pixel is grey. From 1 to 2 floors the colour comes in smoothly. The noise of a weak filter, raised by
      the balance, no longer paints the sky.</li>
      <li><strong>The strength.</strong> The colour’s departure from grey is multiplied by 1.9, the luminance kept, so
      that the nebula’s median chroma in Post’s default display matches the 0.054 measured on the same five composites.
      Where the brightest channel would be shown above 0.9 of the display, the factor is lowered pixel by pixel down to 1:
      gamut compression at constant lightness,<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">[11]</a> so that a bright core is not flattened.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>RGB and LRGB</h2>
    <p>The broadband recipes give the colour as it was recorded. Their colour balance is that of a <a href="/blocks/color/">Color</a> block placed
    after the combination, as in the templates Mono RGB and Mono LRGB: a photometric white balance of the stars, one
    factor per colour.<a class="cite" href="#ref-12">[12]</a> The linear match only puts the channels on one scale.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>RGB</strong> is the red, green and blue channels, matched to the red, and nothing else.</li>
      <li><strong>LRGB</strong> is the colour of RGB and the lightness of L at every pixel. R, G and B are linearly matched to
      L. Each pixel’s three channels are scaled so that its colour ratios keep their own luminance times L, which keeps the
      pixel’s chromaticity on a linear image. Where the colour’s luminance is under its noise floor, the pixel is grey at
      L’s level: the depth of L is kept on the faint areas and on the sky, where the colour has no signal. The colour is
      not smoothed and its strength is not raised.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both hand on to the Color block the saturation map of the stacks of R, G and B, so that the Color block leaves
    out the saturated stars by that map and not by an estimate from the brightest pixels. L is not in it: it scales the
    three channels alike, so a star saturated in L keeps the colour ratios of its R, G and B.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>The shipped recipes</h2>
    <p>A recipe is a file of the application’s folder. It names its inputs, its parameters with their defaults, its steps
    and its output; a file with an error is left out of the list with a message naming the file, the line and the setting.
    When a combination is placed in a project, its recipe is copied into the project, so that the project computes the
    same whatever happens to the file. A project whose copy is older than the file is told so, and changes only when
    the file is applied.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b>The recipes shipped with FLOAT.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Recipe</th><th>Inputs</th><th>Steps</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">S-H-O</th><td>S II, Hα, O III</td><td>Sky; linear match to Hα</td><td>One hue per filter; lightness from the channels, weighted by noise; colour smoothed; strength 1.9</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">H-O</th><td>Hα, O III (two filters, or a dual-band image’s channels)</td><td>Sky; linear match to Hα</td><td>Hues 0° and 180°; same composition as S-H-O</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">RGB</th><td>R, G, B</td><td>Sky; linear match to R</td><td>The channels as they are; saturation maps handed on</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">LRGB</th><td>L, R, G, B</td><td>Sky; linear match to L</td><td>Colour of R, G, B, lightness of L; colour not smoothed; saturation maps handed on</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">HOO (colour camera dual-band)</th><td>Hα read from the red, O III from the mean of green and blue</td><td>Sky; linear match to Hα</td><td>Hα in red, O III in green and blue, as they are; no lightness composition</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">HaRGB</th><td>R, G, B, Hα</td><td>Sky; G and B matched to R; continuum of Hα taken off by R, scale measured on the stars</td><td>The line added to the red, a fifth of it to the blue; saturation maps of R, G, B handed on</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>For HaRGB, the line image is on the red’s scale, a star having the same value in both, so it adds to the
    red. The amount added is the weight of the Hα channel, 1 by default. The HOO recipe reads the H-alpha from the red pixels of a dual-band image and the O III from
    the green and blue ones, as Siril’s extraction does.<a class="cite" href="#ref-13">[13]</a></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s7"><span class="secnum">7</span>Settings</h2>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 2.</b>The settings of the narrow-band blocks. RGB and LRGB have none beyond the channel weights.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">S II hue</th><td class="n">15.5°</td><td class="n">0 – 360</td><td>The colour of the S II filter on the colour wheel (S-H-O only).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Hα hue</th><td class="n">31.4° (S-H-O), 0° (H-O)</td><td class="n">0 – 360</td><td>The colour of the H-alpha filter.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">O III hue</th><td class="n">202.2° (S-H-O), 180° (H-O)</td><td class="n">0 – 360</td><td>The colour of the O III filter.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Lightness</th><td>noise</td><td>noise, equal</td><td>How the filters make the lightness: weighted by their noise, or equally.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Colour smoothing</th><td>Auto</td><td>Auto, Radius</td><td>Auto takes the smoothing radius from the filters’ noise; Radius takes the one set below.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Smoothing radius</th><td class="n">0 px</td><td class="n">0 – 50</td><td>The colour’s smoothing radius when Colour smoothing is Radius; 0: none.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Channel weights</th><td class="n">1</td><td>per channel</td><td>Each channel’s weight, after the balance. A channel too faint for the balance is lowered by hand.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A recipe other than the four shipped types has its own parameters, each with its default and its range, written
    in its file; the block shows them under Recipe settings and refuses a value outside the range.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s8"><span class="secnum">8</span>Limits, and what was tried</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No alignment inside the block.</strong> Inputs that are not on one grid are refused, naming them.
      Align on does the work, before the combination.</li>
      <li><strong>The continuum scale needs stars.</strong> With fewer than 5 isolated, unsaturated stars measured in
      both images, the step stops and asks for the scale to be written in the recipe.</li>
      <li><strong>A background taken from the input’s own sky level was tried and left.</strong> It differs from one
      filter to the other by what the nebula adds, and the balance multiplied the difference into a cast of the whole sky
      (section 3).</li>
      <li><strong>The photometric calibration of stars on a combination.</strong> An estimate of the saturated stars
      from the brightest pixels refused most of a field’s stars on an LRGB (160 of 195); the saturation map of the
      stacks of R, G and B, handed on by the combination, replaces it (129 stars measured).</li>
      <li><strong>No chrominance smoothing in LRGB.</strong> Each filter’s workflow has its own <a href="/blocks/denoise/">Denoise</a>; the combination
      does not smooth the colour and does not raise its strength, which varies by a factor of five from one
      subject to another among broadband composites of professional observatories (nine ESO and ESA/Hubble images).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

